HTTP, as a thing a module provides and other modules use
========================================================

Phase 5 of doc/proposals/006-comunicaciones-unificadas.md.  The core holds
a register; a module serves the HTTP; other modules ask it for routes.

This is the same arrangement as doc/readme.database and doc/readme.cache,
and for the same two reasons.  Modules are RTLD_LOCAL and cannot resolve
each other's symbols, so the core has to be the meeting point between
whoever serves and whoever wants to be served; and holding the requests in
flight in the core is what lets either module be unloaded with some
outstanding.

The core knows nothing about sockets, TLS, chunked encoding or keep-alive.
It knows there is a provider that might not be there, a set of routes
modules have asked for, and a request that is a method, a path, some
headers and a body.


THE TWO SIDES
=============

The **provider** owns the listener, the parsing and its own TLS, and does
the network work on its own thread.  What reaches the main thread is a
request that is already parsed.  It registers with

  http_register_provider(mod, &provider)

and hands the core each request with http_dispatch().  One provider at a
time: a second is refused rather than replacing the first, because two
things owning the same socket is not a state to arrive at silently.

The **consumer** claims routes:

  http_add_route(mod, "POST", "/upload", handle_upload, self)

and checks, when it loads:

  if (!http_available())
    /* say so in the log; switch that part off; carry on */

It does not fail to load and it does not wait.  The part of it that needed
HTTP is off and the rest works.  That is the contract db.h has too, and it
is what keeps "the HTTP module is not loaded" from being an outage.

A route may be claimed before any provider exists.  The module that wants
one loads first as often as not, and making that an error would make load
order part of the configuration.


ROUTING
=======

A path ending in `/` matches everything under it; anything else matches
exactly.  An exact match beats a prefix, and a longer prefix beats a
shorter one:

  /files/index        matches only that
  /files/thumb/       matches /files/thumb/anything
  /files/             matches everything else under /files/

That is the whole routing language, on purpose.  A pattern syntax is a
thing every consumer would have to learn and every provider would have to
agree about, and nothing here has needed one.

The method is part of the route.  Two claims on the same method and path
are refused: two handlers for one route is a question with no right
answer.

Nothing claimed means the core never owned the request: http_dispatch()
says so, the provider sends its own 404, and there is nothing to clean up.


ANSWERING LATER
===============

A handler runs in the main thread and does **not** have to answer there.
Authorising an upload means asking a database, and a handler that blocked
for it would stop every client on the server.  So it is given an
http_req_t and it either

  - fills in the response and returns non-zero, or
  - keeps the identifier, returns zero, and calls http_respond() when the
    answer arrives.

Every request has a deadline -- HTTP_TIMEOUT, five seconds by default --
on a timer of the core's own.  One that passes is answered 504 and a later
answer is dropped.  A client holding a socket open because a module forgot
about it is the failure the deadline exists to bound; answering late is
not a crash, because a module that answers late has been slow rather than
wrong.


WHAT UNLOADING TAKES
====================

A consumer unloading takes its routes, and the requests it will never
answer are handed back to the provider -- hp_cancel() -- so the connection
is let go rather than held to the deadline.

The provider unloading takes every request in flight with it, silently:
there is nobody left to answer through.  The consumers' routes stay, and
wait for another provider.


LIMITS
======

  HTTP_BODY_MAX       65536   what crosses into the main thread
  HTTP_HEADERS_MAX    32      headers in either direction
  HTTP_PATH_MAX       255
  HTTP_ROUTES_MAX     64      per module

HTTP_BODY_MAX is not a limit on what HTTP can transfer.  It is a limit on
what the event loop holds: a file upload is streamed to storage by the
provider and its handler is given the metadata, because a hundred
megabytes buffered here would be a hundred megabytes every other client
waits behind.


WHAT THIS IS FOR
================

File upload and download with object storage behind it, with the ircd
issuing a short-lived token -- HMAC-SHA256 or AES-256-GCM from
include/ircd_sha256.h and include/ircd_aes.h -- bound to the account and
the channel, which the provider validates.  Incoming webhooks and a REST
API for integrations are routes on the same provider.

None of that is in the tree yet.  What is here is the register, which is
the part that has to exist before any of it can be written as a module.
